## Key Analytical Discoveries This Session

### 1. CERT_4 is the Most Significant New Document
CERT-In's July 2025 AIBOM Technical Guidelines represents India's most detailed AI transparency standard. Creates binding AIBOM mandate for government AI procurement; 18 minimum AIBOM elements; VEX documentation required; extends to QBOM/CBOM and HBOM. Creates potential new parallelism layer with SEBI AI reporting regime.
**Flag for P4:** AIBOM creates a new compliance layer for BFSI AI that neither RBI nor SEBI has addressed.

### 2. SEBI_11 Marks Governance Inflection Point
SEBI's 2024–25 Board Memo marks the shift from AI inventory (2019) to AI accountability/responsibility (2024–25). Creates legal duty for AI responsibility applicable to third-party AI tools. No equivalent in RBI framework — cross-regulator gap persists. Cite in P3 discussion section.

### 3. NITI_3 + NITI_6 Confirm fsQCA Evidence
NITI Aayog's 2021 documents explicitly acknowledge the multi-regulator coordination problem and propose CET as the coordination mechanism. CET was never established — definitional Governance Debt. India's governance configuration confirmed: advisory + voluntary self-regulation (general) + mandate for high-risk only.

### 4. Dual-Channel Incident Reporting (M1 Evidence Confirmed)
RBI_1 §27(d) requires CERT-In + RBI notification for cyber incidents. RBI_2 §17(h) requires service provider → RE → RBI within 6 hours. Combined: three-level reporting chain for outsourced IT cyber incidents = strong M1 Manifestation Evidence Layer.

